# Canonical contributor identities.
#
# Git records whatever name and address were configured on the machine that
# made each commit, so one person routinely appears as several contributors:
# a work address and a personal one, a laptop reinstalled with a different
# name, or a commit made through the GitHub web interface, which uses a
# private noreply address rather than the one in your config.
#
# This file states which of those identities are the same person. Git honours
# it in `git shortlog -sne` and `git log --use-mailmap`; GitHub honours it on
# the contributors graph; Reveille honours all four forms of it.
#
# Format, most specific first (see `git help mailmap`):
#
#   Proper Name <proper@address> Commit Name <commit@address>
#   Proper Name <proper@address> <commit@address>
#   <proper@address> <commit@address>
#   Proper Name <proper@address>
#
# It maps identities together. It does not rank anyone, and Reveille's
# ranking is off by default regardless.

# One commit was made through the GitHub web interface, which recorded the
# account's noreply address instead of the configured one. Without this line
# it reads as a separate contributor: the table listed three people where
# there are two, and the charts disagreed with the table about which.
Vara Prasad Chilakanti <varaprasadchilakanti@gmail.com> <140685918+varaprasadchilakanti@users.noreply.github.com>

# The legacy, unprefixed form of the same GitHub noreply address. Accounts
# that predate 2017 have commits under both; this repository has none, and
# the line costs nothing if it never matches.
Vara Prasad Chilakanti <varaprasadchilakanti@gmail.com> <varaprasadchilakanti@users.noreply.github.com>

# The display name was spelled without the space until v0.8.0. The address
# never changed, so Reveille already folds these into one contributor -- this
# line only fixes which spelling is shown.
Vara Prasad Chilakanti <varaprasadchilakanti@gmail.com> Varaprasad Chilakanti <varaprasadchilakanti@gmail.com>
